1
00:00:04,000 --> 00:00:07,755
On the fifth of November, at the Program Management Summit,

2
00:00:07,775 --> 00:00:16,037
every breakout room in the session “Project Manager Role in an AI World” will take on a real project problem with an AI agent.

3
00:00:16,437 --> 00:00:21,808
This briefing explains the situation, what to prepare, and what your room will deliver.

4
00:00:22,908 --> 00:00:26,772
Meet KESTREL: a new sensor-hub chip and its evaluation kit,

5
00:00:26,792 --> 00:00:31,601
fourteen days from tape-out, with a lead customer waiting for samples.

6
00:00:32,001 --> 00:00:35,444
Behind it stands a team across design, verification,

7
00:00:35,464 --> 00:00:38,773
test, software, quality, operations and marketing.

8
00:00:38,793 --> 00:00:43,588
Every one of them needs something from the project manager this week.

9
00:00:44,188 --> 00:00:47,912
On Monday, you inherit KESTREL as its new project manager.

10
00:00:47,932 --> 00:00:51,876
By Thursday morning, you are holding six problems at once.

11
00:00:52,276 --> 00:00:56,921
Sofia forwards a customer change request that nobody can answer yet.

12
00:00:57,171 --> 00:01:02,320
Chen mentions a supplier delay, in a risk log no one has touched in five weeks.

13
00:01:02,570 --> 00:01:08,160
Priya flags a late load board, in a meeting where nobody writes down what was decided.

14
00:01:08,410 --> 00:01:10,283
Marco’s numbers say one thing.

15
00:01:10,303 --> 00:01:13,217
Last week’s status report still says green.

16
00:01:13,467 --> 00:01:18,346
Aiko’s design is not ready, and steering needs a decision by two o’clock.

17
00:01:18,596 --> 00:01:22,462
And the lessons Jonas left behind arrive three days too late.

18
00:01:23,062 --> 00:01:24,632
None of this is unusual.

19
00:01:24,652 --> 00:01:27,634
It is the week most project managers know.

20
00:01:27,884 --> 00:01:33,423
And none of it is the part of the job that matters most: leading a team to good decisions.

21
00:01:34,623 --> 00:01:39,551
Before the summit, we ask for about twenty minutes of preparation, in four steps.

22
00:01:39,901 --> 00:01:40,133
One.

23
00:01:40,153 --> 00:01:44,421
Read “Fourteen Days”, the KESTREL story attached to your invitation.

24
00:01:44,441 --> 00:01:46,532
It takes about twelve minutes.

25
00:01:46,832 --> 00:01:47,088
Two.

26
00:01:47,108 --> 00:01:50,677
Open your room’s case file at the back of the story.

27
00:01:50,697 --> 00:01:54,196
Your invitation tells you which one, from C1 to C6.

28
00:01:54,216 --> 00:01:56,970
Read it as if the problem were yours.

29
00:01:57,270 --> 00:01:57,626
Three.

30
00:01:57,646 --> 00:01:59,192
Find what doesn’t add up.

31
00:01:59,212 --> 00:02:04,203
Every case file hides at least two things a careful project manager would catch.

32
00:02:04,223 --> 00:02:07,868
Note them, and keep them to yourself until the session.

33
00:02:08,168 --> 00:02:08,448
Four.

34
00:02:08,468 --> 00:02:12,829
Bring one real moment from your own work when this problem cost you time,

35
00:02:12,849 --> 00:02:15,029
and a rough estimate of how much.

36
00:02:15,529 --> 00:02:17,694
You don’t need any AI experience.

37
00:02:17,714 --> 00:02:22,416
Rooms mix beginners and experts on purpose, and everyone has a role.

38
00:02:22,566 --> 00:02:25,057
Please don’t bring confidential documents.

39
00:02:25,077 --> 00:02:27,668
Everything you need is in the case file.

40
00:02:28,868 --> 00:02:32,001
On the day, each room takes one of the six moments.

41
00:02:32,101 --> 00:02:36,042
In sixty minutes, you design an agent for it, test it on the case file,

42
00:02:36,062 --> 00:02:38,772
and check whether it catches what you caught.

43
00:02:39,123 --> 00:02:45,746
Your room leaves with a Pilot Pack: the agent’s instructions, the decision the project manager keeps,

44
00:02:45,766 --> 00:02:52,280
one metric, one guardrail, an owner, and a first test to run on a real project after the summit.

45
00:02:53,380 --> 00:02:55,079
The agent takes the routine.

46
00:02:55,099 --> 00:02:57,140
Every decision stays with you.

47
00:02:57,840 --> 00:03:00,511
Read “Fourteen Days” before the fifth of November.

48
00:03:00,531 --> 00:03:02,131
We’ll see you in the room.
